$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid A - λI \\mid &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 - λ & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 - λ & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(2 - λ)\\begin{vmatrix}1 - λ & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(2 - λ)((1 - λ)(1 - λ) - 4) &= 0 \\\\\n(2 - λ)(λ^{2} - 2λ - 3) &= 0 \\\\\n(2 - λ)(λ - 3)(λ + 1) &= 0 \\\\\nλ &= 3, 2,-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3y_1^2 + 2y_2^2 - y_3^2\n$$" 13194139536085 false] [17592186161730 1100 "Given the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=2x_1^2-2x_1x_2+2x_2^2$, find its canonical (diagonal) form $\\mathbf{y}^T \\! D \\mathbf{y}$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $\\mathbf{x}=P\\mathbf{y}$ defines the corresponding change of variables." 13194139536085 false] [17592186161730 1100 "Given the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=2x_1^2-2x_1x_2+2x_2^2,$ find its canonical (diagonal) form $\\mathbf{y}^T \\! D \\mathbf{y}$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $\\mathbf{x}=P\\mathbf{y}$ defines the corresponding change of variables." 13194139536085 true] [17592186161730 1145 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/69dcf821f3ff202b567b901d8f512c5cecf7159617ca51a0664c14a5099e9715.png)\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +3 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For $\\lambda_1=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/fc9465d4e5bcd43a3c29e5946b05ec18cdbc7453a4011aa548f374016c561b45.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/59c1bd46325fab8a52fed162d7ae47e8fb33a10cc524cd770a3b18f5934e51dd.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/adc9241219f3b5ebce0d8418bf34e250b7230ea612fabca5bdfc109c98a18dbb.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/dcab437eb77b29ab5bcd5c3ae14b77f10f01382aafadf2aecded1bb1fb72dc32.png)\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/3f6b1002893290dde6c9a68425b0035e249ff3630a41d29c9da5756c1a18f6a6.png)" 13194139536085 true] [17592186161730 1145 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid A - λI \\mid &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 - λ & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} - 4λ + 3 &= 0 \\\\\nλ &= 3, 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A$.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=3$, we have\n$A - 3I|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} - 3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| = \\sqrt{2}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- For $\\lambda_2=1$, we have\n$A - I|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2 \\| = \\sqrt{2}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139536085 false] [17592186161731 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/6135fe578745ebf2eccb4370557f0dba6ec27361a192e4e87536eadad87a39bf.png)\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $\\mathbf{x}=P\\mathbf{y}$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}$ to the canonical (diagonal) form $\\mathbf{y}^T \\! D \\mathbf{y}.$" 13194139536085 true] [17592186161731 1100 "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $\\mathbf{x}=P\\mathbf{y}$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}$ to the canonical (diagonal) form $\\mathbf{y}^T \\! D \\mathbf{y}$." 13194139536085 false] [17592186161731 1145 "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=4, \\quad\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=2, \\quad\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\quad\\lambda_3=1\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A$.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=4$, we have\n$A - 4I|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} - 4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| = \\sqrt{2}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- For $\\lambda_2=2$, we have\n$A - 2I|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} - 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2 \\| = \\sqrt{2}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- For $\\lambda_3=1$, we have\n$A - I|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_3$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_3 \\| = 1$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139536085 false] [17592186161731 1145 "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=4, \\quad$ $\\lambda_2=2, \\quad$ and $\\quad\\lambda_3=1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For $\\lambda_1=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/13eee082d48c56e6c7e796549eddc0185868b39841a7c5946ce9ce9e996ba553.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/1e7c81e373377bd719e1bbc9e87a8c88d13f6116bcf2949897fc26246e2265d1.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/61311029da90b54253a552ce2198ce9193d7f343c3ab345eea25419444da00a3.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/3bf5334469b8b135548dfbd0c35725ee6927e2a169c6db37a1b0bae3f62695e5.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_3=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/1cb1b34b0df191e9fa63a907ba59e26a2ad16f3977e97d5b8588dc5e51f2c3a0.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_3$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_3  \\| = 1,$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/39bf82ed6be834f41140a70e048268b39d47d7a0944461247d312d4efe95d184.png)\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n![](/home